      "text": "To factor the expression \\(9x^2 + 3x\\), we start by identifying the greatest common factor (GCF) of the terms \\(9x^2\\) and \\(3x\\). The GCF of the coefficients 9 and 3 is 3, and the GCF of the variable parts \\(x^2\\) and \\(x\\) is \\(x\\). Therefore, the GCF of the entire expression is \\(3x\\).\n\nNext, we factor out the GCF \\(3x\\) from each term in the expression:\n\\[9x^2 + 3x = 3x(3x) + 3x(1) = 3x(3x + 1).\\]\n\nThus, the completely factored form of the expression is:\n\\[\\boxed{3x(3x + 1)}.\\]",
